            <ArticleTitle>Effect of prior administration of cold saline on pain during propofol injection. A comparison with cold propofol and propofol with lignocaine.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1069-70</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A single-blind, randomised, controlled study was undertaken to compare the efficacy of three methods of preventing pain during injection of propofol on induction of anaesthesia. Patients were allocated randomly to receive unmodified propofol, propofol with 0.05% lignocaine, propofol at 4 degrees C and unmodified propofol preceded by 10 ml of 0.9% saline at 4 degrees C. Prior injection of cold saline reduced the incidence of pain and discomfort significantly (22%) compared with unmodified propofol (75%; p less than 0.005) and was similar to that after cold propofol (33%) and propofol with lignocaine (44%). There was no significant difference between the treatment groups.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
